### How does a batch engine work?

A batch engine like Spark batch reads an entire bounded dataset, builds an optimized plan through Catalyst, runs it across executors, writes the result, and stops until the next scheduled run. Because it sees all the data, it does large joins and full aggregations efficiently and is trivial to re-run.

```python
# Spark batch: process the whole day at once
df = spark.read.parquet(
    "s3://national-bank-lake/raw/txns/dt=2024-10-29/")

result = df.groupBy("account_id") \
    .agg({"amount": "sum"}) \
    .orderBy("sum(amount)", ascending=False)

result.write.parquet(
    "s3://national-bank-lake/curated/daily_summary/")
```

```text
+----------+-----------+
|account_id|sum(amount)|
+----------+-----------+
|   CUR3002| 1250000.00|
|   SAV2001|  450000.00|
+----------+-----------+
```

### How does Spark Structured Streaming (micro-batch) work?

Spark Structured Streaming uses micro-batch: it treats the stream as a series of small bounded batches, triggering every short interval. Under the hood it reuses Spark's batch engine and Catalyst optimizer on tiny slices. This gives high throughput and the familiar DataFrame API, but carries a latency floor equal to the trigger interval.

```python
# Spark Structured Streaming: micro-batch on Kafka
df = spark.readStream.format("kafka") \
    .option("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "kafka:9092") \
    .option("subscribe", "bank-transactions") \
    .load()

from pyspark.sql.functions import window, col, sum as _sum

agg = df.groupBy(
    window(col("timestamp"), "1 minute"),
    col("account_id")
).agg(_sum("amount").alias("total"))

agg.writeStream \
    .outputMode("update") \
    .format("delta") \
    .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/txn_agg") \
    .trigger(processingTime="2 seconds") \
    .start()
# Latency floor: ~2 seconds
```

### How does Flink (record-at-a-time) work?

Flink uses a true record-at-a-time model: each event flows through operators individually, so latency can be in the low milliseconds. This gives precise event-time handling, rich keyed state updated per event, and exactly-once via checkpoint barriers.

