### How do the three engines compare?

| Aspect | Spark Batch | Spark Structured Streaming | Flink |
|---|---|---|---|
| Model | Bounded, scheduled | Micro-batch (tiny slices) | Record-at-a-time |
| Latency | Minutes to hours | Hundreds of ms to seconds | Low milliseconds |
| Throughput | Very high | Very high | High |
| API | DataFrame, SQL | Same as batch | DataStream, Table API |
| Optimizer | Catalyst + Tungsten | Reuses Catalyst | Custom streaming optimizer |
| State | None (re-read) | Managed state store | RocksDB / memory backend |
| Fault tolerance | Re-run job | Checkpoints + WAL | Checkpoint barriers |
| Best for | Reports, backfills | Near-real-time dashboards | Fraud, real-time scoring |

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class EngineComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Spark Batch: whole day, nightly, cheap");
        System.out.println("Spark Streaming: micro-batch, ~2s latency");
        System.out.println("Flink: per-event, ms latency, complex");
    }
}
```

```text
Spark Batch: whole day, nightly, cheap
Spark Streaming: micro-batch, ~2s latency
Flink: per-event, ms latency, complex
```

### When do you use micro-batch vs record-at-a-time?

Use micro-batch when near-real-time of a second or two is good enough and you value one unified API and operational familiarity. Use record-at-a-time when you need the lowest latency, sophisticated per-event stateful logic, or the strongest event-time semantics.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class WhenToChoose {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Micro-batch (Spark Streaming):");
        System.out.println("  Near-real-time dashboards");
        System.out.println("  Enrichment pipelines");
        System.out.println("  Teams already using Spark batch");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Record-at-a-time (Flink):");
        System.out.println("  Fraud detection (block in ms)");
        System.out.println("  Complex event processing (CEP)");
        System.out.println("  Precise event-time windowing");
        System.out.println("  Large keyed state (RocksDB)");
    }
}
```

```text
Micro-batch (Spark Streaming):
  Near-real-time dashboards
  Enrichment pipelines
  Teams already using Spark batch

Record-at-a-time (Flink):
  Fraud detection (block in ms)
  Complex event processing (CEP)
  Precise event-time windowing
  Large keyed state (RocksDB)
```

### How has the line blurred between engines?

Spark narrowed its latency with a continuous processing mode, and Flink supports batch over bounded streams, so the decision often comes down to your latency budget, how complex your state is, and which engine your team already operates well.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class BlurredLines {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Spark moving toward streaming:");
        System.out.println("  Continuous mode (experimental)");
        System.out.println("  Shorter trigger intervals");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Flink moving toward batch:");
        System.out.println("  BATCH execution mode for bounded data");
        System.out.println("  Unified DataStream + Table API");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Decision factors:");
        System.out.println("  1. Latency budget");
        System.out.println("  2. State complexity");
        System.out.println("  3. Team familiarity");
    }
}
```

```text
Spark moving toward streaming:
  Continuous mode (experimental)
  Shorter trigger intervals

Flink moving toward batch:
  BATCH execution mode for bounded data
  Unified DataStream + Table API

Decision factors:
  1. Latency budget
  2. State complexity
  3. Team familiarity
```

### What does the bank use in practice?

A bank typically runs batch for reporting, micro-batch for most near-real-time dashboards and enrichment, and reserves Flink for the latency-critical, heavily stateful fraud and risk jobs. Matching the engine to the workload keeps the platform both cost-effective and fast where it matters.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class BankPractice {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("National Bank engine allocation:");
        System.out.println("  Spark Batch: nightly reports, backfills");
        System.out.println("  Spark Streaming: near-RT dashboards");
        System.out.println("  Flink: fraud detection, risk scoring");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Result: cost-effective + fast where needed.");
    }
}
```

```text
National Bank engine allocation:
  Spark Batch: nightly reports, backfills
  Spark Streaming: near-RT dashboards
  Flink: fraud detection, risk scoring

Result: cost-effective + fast where needed.
```

